  "family": "Ulmaceae",
  "memo": "&lt;em&gt;Ulmus parvifolia ‘Drake&lt;/em&gt;,’ commonly known as the Drake elm, is a cultivar of the Chinese elm selected for its vigorous growth and dense canopy. Additionally, this cultivar tolerates warm temperatures better than many others. While the species is deciduous in cooler climates, ‘Drake’ is semi-evergreen in warmer regions. This cultivar is highly valued for its strong resistance to Dutch elm disease, tolerance of elm leaf beetle, and adaptability to urban conditions, including heat, drought, compacted soils, and air pollution. Widely planted as a street tree, shade tree, and park specimen throughout the southern United States, Australia, and Mediterranean climates, it requires minimal pruning but benefits from structural training when young to develop strong branching. In storm-prone areas, occasional thinning may be needed to reduce wind resistance.",
